message.content:
Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** recipe that shows how to turn a fully‑configured `RootObjectMapper` into its XContent representation – i.e., the JSON that ES stores under `_mapping`.  
All four “default” flags (`dynamic_date_formats`, `dynamic_templates`, `date_detection`, `numeric_detection`) are already baked into the mapper when you build it, so all you really need to decide about at serialisation time is *whether* to emit those defaults and *whether* to omit runtime fields.

---

### 1️⃣ What you’ll need

| Item | Why |
|------|-----|
| `org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.XContentBuilder` | Builds the raw JSON stream |
| `org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.RootObjectMapper` | Your top‑level mapper instance |
| `org.elasticsearch.common.settings.Settings` | Optional – used only if your mapper was created programmatically |

If you’re working inside an existing plugin / test harness you probably already have access to the above types.

---

### 2️⃣ Build the `XContentBuilder`

```java
import org.apache.lucene.util.BytesRef;
import org.elasticsearch.common.bytes.BytesReference;
import org.elasticsearch.common.io.stream.Writeable.Reader;
import org.elasticsearch.common.xcontent.*;
import org.elasticsearch.index.mapper.*;

...

// Create a JSON builder backed by a byte buffer
BytesArray bytesArray = new BytesArray(new byte[0]);          // empty start
ByteBufferOutputStream out = new ByteBufferOutputStream(bytesArray.buffer());
XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.builder(XContentFactory.JSON)
                                         .stream(out);           // <-- this line creates the builder
```

*(In most real projects you’d just do `XContentBuilder builder = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder()` – the extra plumbing shown above keeps the example self‑contained.)*

---

### 3️⃣ Prepare the *Params* argument

Elasticsearch’s `doXContent(...)` accepts a `Params` implementation that tells the mapper what to output.  
Because the public API doesn’t expose a concrete class, we usually hand over a simple anonymous inner class or a lambda.  

For clarity we’ll construct a tiny helper that implements `Params` inline:

```java
class SimpleParams implements Mapper.Params {

    private final boolean includeDefaults;
    private final boolean skipRuntimeFields;

    SimpleParams(boolean includeDefaults, boolean skipRuntimeFields) {
        this.includeDefaults = includeDefaults;
        this.skipRuntimeFields = skipRuntimeFields;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean includeDefaults() { return includeDefaults; }
    @Override
    public boolean skipRuntimeFields() { return skipRuntimeFields; }
}
```

Now instantiate it with the exact behaviour you asked for:

```java
SimpleParams params = new SimpleParams(/*includeDefaults=*/true,
                                      /*skipRuntimeFields=*/true);
```

Setting `includeDefaults=true` guarantees that the generated JSON contains entries such as `"dynamic_date_formats" : [...]`,
while `skipRuntimeFields=true` omits any field definitions marked as runtime.

---

### 4️⃣ Call `doXContent`

With everything wired up, the actual serialisation step looks almost trivial:

```java
// Assume `mapper` is your pre‑built RootObjectMapper instance:
mapper.doXContent(builder, params);

// Flush & close the builder to finish writing
builder.close();

// Grab the resulting JSON string
byte[] jsonBytes = ((BytesReference)out.get()).bytes();
String jsonMapping = new String(jsonBytes, StandardCharsets.UTF_8);

System.out.println("Generated Mapping:\n" + jsonMapping);
```

That last block prints the full mapping document exactly as ES would store it internally.

---

### 5️⃣ Quick sanity‑check

Run the snippet against a freshly built mapper that declares each of the four features:

```java
RootObjectMapper mapper = RootObjectMapper.Builder.newBuilder()
    .name("_doc")
    .properties(Map.of(
        "_id", TextFieldMapper.NAME,
        "timestamp", DateFieldMapper.DATE_TYPE_NAME))
    .build();

mapper.setDynamicDateFormats(List.of("epoch_millis"));            // <– dynamic_date_formats
mapper.addDynamicTemplate(DynamicTemplates.Template.from("*", "*")); // <– dynamic_templates
mapper.dateDetection(true);                                       // <– date_detection
mapper.numericDetection(false);                                  // <– numeric_detection
```

After running the serialiser described earlier, you should see keys like:

```json
{
  "mappings": {
    "_source": {...},
    "dynamic_date_formats": [...],
    "dynamic_templates": [...],
    "date_detection": true,
    "numeric_detection": false,
    ...
  }
}
```

…and no entry named `"runtime"` thanks to `skipRuntimeFields`.

---

#### TL;DR

```java
XContentBuilder b = XContentFactory.jsonBuilder();
b.startObject();
// …your mapper logic…
mapper.doXContent(b, new SimpleParams(true, true));
b.endObject();
b.flush();
String json = b.string();   // ← the desired XContent
```

Just replace the placeholder comments with whatever customisations you’ve applied to the `RootObjectMapper`; the rest stays identical. Happy mapping!

